  "worked_solution": "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-(-2)(1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +42 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -7)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(7)\\,\u2295\\,{J}_{1}(6)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
